21:21:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It would certainly be wonderful if biology worked that way, and logically, it makes sense that a sick body *should* want to accept a healthy new heart or kidney. But the reality is that the body’s defense system is completely blind to whether something is helpful or harmful. Think of your immune system like a highly aggressive security guard that only has one rule: **If it doesn't have your exact DNA, destroy it.** This rule is actually what keeps you alive every day. If the human body naturally accepted foreign cells, we wouldn't have an immune system. We would have no defense against viruses, bacteria, parasites, or infections. The body would just "accept" the flu virus or a bacterial infection the same way you are suggesting it accepts a transplanted organ. Because the immune system can't tell the difference between a deadly bacteria and a life-saving kidney—it just sees that both are "foreign"—it attacks both with the exact same lethal force. We know for a fact that bodies do not naturally accept new parts because of what happens when transplant patients stop taking their medication. If a person with a transplanted kidney stops taking their daily immunosuppressant pills, their immune system will "wake up," realize there is a foreign object in the body, and destroy the transplanted kidney, usually within a matter of weeks. While I understand you have a different view on this, the entire field of transplant medicine, the history of surgery, and the daily lived reality of hundreds of thousands of transplant recipients worldwide demonstrate that the body's natural instinct is always to reject foreign tissue.
